{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "threesock",
          "semanticVersion": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/threesock/threesock",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "a1.1/autoscaling",
              "name": "Autoscaling",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Autoscaling or a capacity plan is defined"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Workloads autoscale on load, or a documented capacity plan sets headroom targets."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a HorizontalPodAutoscaler or cloud autoscaling policy, or document a capacity plan with headroom targets.\n\nRisk: Traffic spikes (or abuse) exhaust fixed capacity and cause an outage.",
                "markdown": "**A1.1**: Workloads autoscale on load, or a documented capacity plan sets headroom targets.\n\n**Risk:** Traffic spikes (or abuse) exhaust fixed capacity and cause an outage.\n\n**Fix:** Add a HorizontalPodAutoscaler or cloud autoscaling policy, or document a capacity plan with headroom targets.\n\nExample:\n\n```\napiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nspec: { minReplicas: 2, maxReplicas: 10, metrics: [{ type: Resource, resource: { name: cpu, target: { type: Utilization, averageUtilization: 70 } } }] }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "A1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.1/load-tests",
              "name": "LoadTests",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load or performance tests exist"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Load tests (k6, Artillery, Locust, Gatling, ...) exist and run before major releases."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a load test for the critical paths and run it before major releases.\n\nRisk: Capacity limits are discovered in production, during the busiest hour.",
                "markdown": "**A1.1**: Load tests (k6, Artillery, Locust, Gatling, ...) exist and run before major releases.\n\n**Risk:** Capacity limits are discovered in production, during the busiest hour.\n\n**Fix:** Add a load test for the critical paths and run it before major releases.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport http from 'k6/http';\nexport const options = { vus: 50, duration: '2m' };\nexport default () => http.get(`${__ENV.BASE_URL}/health`);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "A1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.1/resource-limits",
              "name": "ResourceLimits",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workloads declare resource limits"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every container and task declares CPU and memory limits (and requests where the scheduler uses them)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set requests and limits on every container, and memory/CPU on every task definition or compose service.\n\nRisk: One runaway workload can starve its neighbours and take down unrelated services.",
                "markdown": "**A1.1**: Every container and task declares CPU and memory limits (and requests where the scheduler uses them).\n\n**Risk:** One runaway workload can starve its neighbours and take down unrelated services.\n\n**Fix:** Set requests and limits on every container, and memory/CPU on every task definition or compose service.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresources:\n  requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\n  limits:   { cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.2/backups",
              "name": "Backups",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Persistent data stores have automated backups"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every database has automated backups with a retention policy, and point-in-time recovery where supported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable automated backups with explicit retention (and PITR), or cover the stores with an AWS Backup plan.\n\nRisk: A bad migration, deletion or ransomware event with no backup is permanent data loss.",
                "markdown": "**A1.2**: Every database has automated backups with a retention policy, and point-in-time recovery where supported.\n\n**Risk:** A bad migration, deletion or ransomware event with no backup is permanent data loss.\n\n**Fix:** Enable automated backups with explicit retention (and PITR), or cover the stores with an AWS Backup plan.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  backup_retention_period = 14\n}\nresource \"aws_dynamodb_table\" \"events\" {\n  point_in_time_recovery { enabled = true }\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "A1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.2/deletion-protection",
              "name": "DeletionProtection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Databases have deletion protection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Production databases enable deletion protection so a mistaken apply or console click cannot drop them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable deletion protection on databases (and turn it off deliberately when decommissioning).\n\nRisk: One bad `terraform apply` or a compromised credential deletes the database outright.",
                "markdown": "**A1.2**: Production databases enable deletion protection so a mistaken apply or console click cannot drop them.\n\n**Risk:** One bad `terraform apply` or a compromised credential deletes the database outright.\n\n**Fix:** Enable deletion protection on databases (and turn it off deliberately when decommissioning).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.2/health-checks",
              "name": "HealthChecks",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workloads have health checks for self-healing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every workload declares liveness/readiness probes (or a HEALTHCHECK / load balancer health check) so failures are detected and replaced automatically."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add readiness and liveness probes to containers (or HEALTHCHECK / target group health checks).\n\nRisk: A hung process keeps receiving traffic and is never restarted.",
                "markdown": "**A1.2**: Every workload declares liveness/readiness probes (or a HEALTHCHECK / load balancer health check) so failures are detected and replaced automatically.\n\n**Risk:** A hung process keeps receiving traffic and is never restarted.\n\n**Fix:** Add readiness and liveness probes to containers (or HEALTHCHECK / target group health checks).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nreadinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 }, periodSeconds: 10 }\nlivenessProbe:  { httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 }, initialDelaySeconds: 15 }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.2/multi-az",
              "name": "MultiAz",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Critical data stores are deployed across availability zones"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Databases and caches run in more than one availability zone with automatic failover."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable Multi-AZ (RDS), automatic failover (ElastiCache) or regional availability (Cloud SQL).\n\nRisk: A single-zone failure takes the database down until the zone recovers.",
                "markdown": "**A1.2**: Databases and caches run in more than one availability zone with automatic failover.\n\n**Risk:** A single-zone failure takes the database down until the zone recovers.\n\n**Fix:** Enable Multi-AZ (RDS), automatic failover (ElastiCache) or regional availability (Cloud SQL).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  multi_az = true\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.3/restore-tests",
              "name": "RestoreTests",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Backup restores and DR exercises are tested and recorded"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Backup restores are tested on a schedule (quarterly for critical systems), with dated results kept."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Run and record a restore test each quarter: date, what was restored, time taken, and issues found.\n\nRisk: Untested backups routinely fail when needed: wrong scope, missing keys, or restores slower than the RTO.",
                "markdown": "**A1.3**: Backup restores are tested on a schedule (quarterly for critical systems), with dated results kept.\n\n**Risk:** Untested backups routinely fail when needed: wrong scope, missing keys, or restores slower than the RTO.\n\n**Fix:** Run and record a restore test each quarter: date, what was restored, time taken, and issues found.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Restore test log\n| Date | System | Result | Time | Follow-up |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-09-30 | Postgres | Pass | 42 min | none |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1.3/rto-rpo",
              "name": "RtoRpo",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RTO and RPO are defined"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Recovery time and recovery point objectives are defined for each system."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set RTO and RPO per system in the DR plan and design backups and failover to meet them.\n\nRisk: Without objectives, nobody can tell whether backups and failover are adequate until it is too late.",
                "markdown": "**A1.3**: Recovery time and recovery point objectives are defined for each system.\n\n**Risk:** Without objectives, nobody can tell whether backups and failover are adequate until it is too late.\n\n**Fix:** Set RTO and RPO per system in the DR plan and design backups and failover to meet them.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| System | RTO | RPO |\n|---|---|---|\n| API | 1 hour | 15 minutes |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "A1.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "A1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1.1/data-classification",
              "name": "DataClassification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A data classification scheme is defined"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A classification scheme (e.g. public / internal / confidential / restricted) is defined with handling rules, and applied to data stores and fields."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Define classification levels and their handling rules, then tag data stores and sensitive fields.\n\nRisk: Without classification, confidential data is handled like everything else: logged, copied to lower environments and shared with vendors.",
                "markdown": "**C1.1**: A classification scheme (e.g. public / internal / confidential / restricted) is defined with handling rules, and applied to data stores and fields.\n\n**Risk:** Without classification, confidential data is handled like everything else: logged, copied to lower environments and shared with vendors.\n\n**Fix:** Define classification levels and their handling rules, then tag data stores and sensitive fields.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Level | Examples | Handling |\n|---|---|---|\n| Restricted | Card data, credentials | Encrypted fields, no logs, no lower environments |\n| Confidential | Customer PII | Encrypted at rest, access logged |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "C1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "C1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1.1/encryption-at-rest",
              "name": "EncryptionAtRest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data stores are encrypted at rest"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every database, volume, file system and cache is encrypted at rest, preferably with managed KMS keys."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable encryption at rest on every data store (it usually requires a replacement for existing RDS instances: snapshot, copy encrypted, restore).\n\nRisk: Unencrypted storage exposes data through snapshots, decommissioned disks and backup copies.",
                "markdown": "**C1.1**: Every database, volume, file system and cache is encrypted at rest, preferably with managed KMS keys.\n\n**Risk:** Unencrypted storage exposes data through snapshots, decommissioned disks and backup copies.\n\n**Fix:** Enable encryption at rest on every data store (it usually requires a replacement for existing RDS instances: snapshot, copy encrypted, restore).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  storage_encrypted = true\n  kms_key_id        = aws_kms_key.data.arn\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "C1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "C1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1.1/pii-in-fixtures",
              "name": "PiiInFixtures",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Fixtures and seed data contain no real personal or card data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Seeds, fixtures and sample data are synthetic: test card numbers, reserved example domains, generated identifiers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace real-looking data with synthetic values (example.com addresses, provider test cards) or generate it with Faker; purge any real data from history.\n\nRisk: Real customer data in the repository is a breach of its own, copied to every clone, CI runner and laptop.",
                "markdown": "**C1.1**: Seeds, fixtures and sample data are synthetic: test card numbers, reserved example domains, generated identifiers.\n\n**Risk:** Real customer data in the repository is a breach of its own, copied to every clone, CI runner and laptop.\n\n**Fix:** Replace real-looking data with synthetic values (example.com addresses, provider test cards) or generate it with Faker; purge any real data from history.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n{ \"email\": \"ada@example.com\", \"card\": \"4242424242424242\", \"ssn\": \"000-00-0000\" }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "C1.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "C1.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1.2/lifecycle-rules",
              "name": "LifecycleRules",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Retention is enforced by lifecycle rules and TTLs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Buckets have lifecycle expiration, log groups have a retention period, and ephemeral tables use TTLs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add lifecycle expiration to buckets and retention_in_days to log groups, matching your retention schedule.\n\nRisk: Without automated deletion, data (and logs full of it) accumulates forever, enlarging every breach.",
                "markdown": "**C1.2**: Buckets have lifecycle expiration, log groups have a retention period, and ephemeral tables use TTLs.\n\n**Risk:** Without automated deletion, data (and logs full of it) accumulates forever, enlarging every breach.\n\n**Fix:** Add lifecycle expiration to buckets and retention_in_days to log groups, matching your retention schedule.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration\" \"exports\" {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.exports.id\n  rule {\n    id     = \"expire-exports\"\n    status = \"Enabled\"\n    expiration { days = 30 }\n  }\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "C1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "C1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1.2/retention-schedule",
              "name": "RetentionSchedule",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Retention periods are defined per data class"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Retention periods are defined per data class, and say how data is deleted at the end of them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Document a retention schedule per data class and implement it with TTLs, lifecycle rules or purge jobs.\n\nRisk: Data kept forever is breach exposure without business value, and violates privacy commitments.",
                "markdown": "**C1.2**: Retention periods are defined per data class, and say how data is deleted at the end of them.\n\n**Risk:** Data kept forever is breach exposure without business value, and violates privacy commitments.\n\n**Fix:** Document a retention schedule per data class and implement it with TTLs, lifecycle rules or purge jobs.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Data | Retention | Deletion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Application logs | 90 days hot, 12 months archive | Bucket lifecycle rule |\n| Closed accounts | 30 days | Nightly purge job |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "C1.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "C1.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc1.3/codeowners",
              "name": "Codeowners",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CODEOWNERS assigns an owner to every path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A CODEOWNERS file assigns an owner to every directory, with named teams on security-sensitive paths."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add .github/CODEOWNERS with a catch-all owner and named teams for sensitive paths, then require code owner review in branch protection.\n\nRisk: Changes to unowned code can merge without review from anyone accountable for it.",
                "markdown": "**CC1.3**: A CODEOWNERS file assigns an owner to every directory, with named teams on security-sensitive paths.\n\n**Risk:** Changes to unowned code can merge without review from anyone accountable for it.\n\n**Fix:** Add .github/CODEOWNERS with a catch-all owner and named teams for sensitive paths, then require code owner review in branch protection.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# .github/CODEOWNERS\n*                 @your-org/engineering\n/src/auth/        @your-org/security\n/infra/           @your-org/platform\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC1.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC1.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc2.1/observability",
              "name": "Observability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Monitoring and dashboards are defined as code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Error tracking, metrics and dashboards are configured in code (Sentry, OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog, CloudWatch, ...)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Instrument the service (error tracking and metrics) and keep dashboards and monitors in code.\n\nRisk: Without instrumentation, security-relevant failures and abuse are invisible until users report them.",
                "markdown": "**CC2.1**: Error tracking, metrics and dashboards are configured in code (Sentry, OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog, CloudWatch, ...).\n\n**Risk:** Without instrumentation, security-relevant failures and abuse are invisible until users report them.\n\n**Fix:** Instrument the service (error tracking and metrics) and keep dashboards and monitors in code.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport * as Sentry from '@sentry/node';\nSentry.init({ dsn: process.env.SENTRY_DSN, tracesSampleRate: 0.1 });\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC2.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC2.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc2.1/sbom",
              "name": "Sbom",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CI generates a software bill of materials"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "CI produces an SBOM (CycloneDX or SPDX) for each release or build."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Generate an SBOM in CI with Syft or a CycloneDX tool and keep it with the release artifacts.\n\nRisk: Without an SBOM you cannot answer \"are we affected?\" quickly when a new vulnerability lands.",
                "markdown": "**CC2.1**: CI produces an SBOM (CycloneDX or SPDX) for each release or build.\n\n**Risk:** Without an SBOM you cannot answer \"are we affected?\" quickly when a new vulnerability lands.\n\n**Fix:** Generate an SBOM in CI with Syft or a CycloneDX tool and keep it with the release artifacts.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- uses: anchore/sbom-action@<sha>  # pin to a commit SHA\n  with: { format: cyclonedx-json, output-file: sbom.cdx.json }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC2.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC2.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc2.2/pr-template-security",
              "name": "PrTemplateSecurity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The pull request template has a security section"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PR template asks authors to state the security impact of each change."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a security section with a short checklist to .github/pull_request_template.md.\n\nRisk: Without a prompt, security-relevant changes (new endpoints, data flows, dependencies) go through review unflagged.",
                "markdown": "**CC2.2**: The PR template asks authors to state the security impact of each change.\n\n**Risk:** Without a prompt, security-relevant changes (new endpoints, data flows, dependencies) go through review unflagged.\n\n**Fix:** Add a security section with a short checklist to .github/pull_request_template.md.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Security impact\n- [ ] Touches auth, permissions, secrets or personal data\n- [ ] Adds a dependency or third-party service\n- [ ] Changes infrastructure, network or IAM\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC2.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC2.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc2.2/security-docs-linked",
              "name": "SecurityDocsLinked",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security policies and runbooks are linked from the README"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The README links to the security policy and the security documentation, so people can find them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a Security section to the README linking SECURITY.md and your security docs folder.\n\nRisk: Documentation nobody can find is not followed.",
                "markdown": "**CC2.2**: The README links to the security policy and the security documentation, so people can find them.\n\n**Risk:** Documentation nobody can find is not followed.\n\n**Fix:** Add a Security section to the README linking SECURITY.md and your security docs folder.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Security\nSee [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) to report a vulnerability, and [docs/security](docs/security) for policies and runbooks.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC2.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC2.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc2.3/security-policy",
              "name": "SecurityPolicy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Vulnerability disclosure policy with a reporting channel"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A SECURITY.md (or /.well-known/security.txt) tells external researchers how to report a vulnerability and what response to expect."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add SECURITY.md at the repository root with a private reporting channel, supported versions and expected response time.\n\nRisk: Without a published channel, vulnerabilities get reported publicly, to the wrong person, or not at all.",
                "markdown": "**CC2.3**: A SECURITY.md (or /.well-known/security.txt) tells external researchers how to report a vulnerability and what response to expect.\n\n**Risk:** Without a published channel, vulnerabilities get reported publicly, to the wrong person, or not at all.\n\n**Fix:** Add SECURITY.md at the repository root with a private reporting channel, supported versions and expected response time.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# Security policy\n\n## Reporting a vulnerability\nEmail security@example.com or use GitHub private vulnerability reporting.\nWe acknowledge reports within 2 business days and aim to fix Critical issues within 7 days.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC2.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC2.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc3.2/adrs",
              "name": "Adrs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Architecture decision records cover security considerations"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Significant architectural decisions are recorded as ADRs, and ADRs discuss their security considerations."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Record decisions in docs/adr/NNNN-title.md with a \"Security considerations\" section.\n\nRisk: Without recorded decisions, the security reasoning behind an architecture is lost and gets undone by later changes.",
                "markdown": "**CC3.2**: Significant architectural decisions are recorded as ADRs, and ADRs discuss their security considerations.\n\n**Risk:** Without recorded decisions, the security reasoning behind an architecture is lost and gets undone by later changes.\n\n**Fix:** Record decisions in docs/adr/NNNN-title.md with a \"Security considerations\" section.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# 0003. Use OIDC for CI cloud access\n## Status\nAccepted\n## Decision\nCI assumes a deploy role through OIDC.\n## Security considerations\nNo long-lived keys in CI; role scoped to the deploy bucket.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC3.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC3.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc3.2/risk-register",
              "name": "RiskRegister",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A risk register records likelihood, impact, owner and treatment"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Risks are recorded with likelihood, impact, an owner and a treatment decision, and link to tracking issues."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep a risk register (a Markdown table is fine) and review it at least quarterly.\n\nRisk: Unrecorded risks have no owner, so they are neither fixed nor consciously accepted.",
                "markdown": "**CC3.2**: Risks are recorded with likelihood, impact, an owner and a treatment decision, and link to tracking issues.\n\n**Risk:** Unrecorded risks have no owner, so they are neither fixed nor consciously accepted.\n\n**Fix:** Keep a risk register (a Markdown table is fine) and review it at least quarterly.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Owner | Treatment |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Leaked deploy key | Low | High | @platform | Mitigate: OIDC, no stored keys |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC3.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC3.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc3.2/threat-model",
              "name": "ThreatModel",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A threat model exists for the system"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A threat model names the assets, trust boundaries, threats and mitigations for the system, and is updated with major changes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Write a short threat model (docs/security/threat-model.md): what you protect, where untrusted input enters, what could go wrong (STRIDE works), and what stops it.\n\nRisk: Without one, security work follows whatever is noticed rather than what matters most, and design flaws ship unexamined.",
                "markdown": "**CC3.2**: A threat model names the assets, trust boundaries, threats and mitigations for the system, and is updated with major changes.\n\n**Risk:** Without one, security work follows whatever is noticed rather than what matters most, and design flaws ship unexamined.\n\n**Fix:** Write a short threat model (docs/security/threat-model.md): what you protect, where untrusted input enters, what could go wrong (STRIDE works), and what stops it.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# Threat model\n## Assets\nCustomer PII in Postgres; API keys for payment providers.\n## Trust boundaries\nPublic API, admin console, webhook endpoint.\n## Threats and mitigations\n| Threat | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Forged webhook | Signature verification |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC3.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC3.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc3.3/auth-bypass",
              "name": "AuthBypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No authentication bypass flags or hard-coded privileged users"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Authentication cannot be switched off by a flag or environment variable, and no code grants privileges to a hard-coded username."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the bypass. For local development, use a seeded test user against the real auth path, and authorize by role, never by username.\n\nRisk: A bypass meant for debugging reaches production sooner or later, giving anyone who finds it full access.",
                "markdown": "**CC3.3**: Authentication cannot be switched off by a flag or environment variable, and no code grants privileges to a hard-coded username.\n\n**Risk:** A bypass meant for debugging reaches production sooner or later, giving anyone who finds it full access.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the bypass. For local development, use a seeded test user against the real auth path, and authorize by role, never by username.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nif (!session.user.roles.includes('admin')) return res.sendStatus(403);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC3.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC3.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc3.4/manifest-ownership",
              "name": "ManifestOwnership",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "New dependencies and infrastructure changes need a named reviewer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Dependency manifests, Dockerfiles and IaC have specific CODEOWNERS, so adding a dependency, service or datastore is reviewed by someone accountable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Give manifests, Dockerfiles and infrastructure directories specific CODEOWNERS and require code owner review.\n\nRisk: New SDKs and services slip in through routine reviews, bringing data flows and vulnerabilities nobody assessed.",
                "markdown": "**CC3.4**: Dependency manifests, Dockerfiles and IaC have specific CODEOWNERS, so adding a dependency, service or datastore is reviewed by someone accountable.\n\n**Risk:** New SDKs and services slip in through routine reviews, bringing data flows and vulnerabilities nobody assessed.\n\n**Fix:** Give manifests, Dockerfiles and infrastructure directories specific CODEOWNERS and require code owner review.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n/package.json   @your-org/platform\n/infra/         @your-org/platform\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC3.4"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC3.4"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.1/iac-scanning",
              "name": "IacScanning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Infrastructure as code is scanned in CI"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Terraform, Kubernetes, CloudFormation and Dockerfiles are scanned for misconfigurations (Checkov, Trivy config, tfsec, hadolint, ...) on every change."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Run Checkov or Trivy in config mode on pull requests that touch infrastructure.\n\nRisk: Misconfigurations such as public buckets or open ports are the leading cause of cloud breaches, and are cheap to catch before apply.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.1**: Terraform, Kubernetes, CloudFormation and Dockerfiles are scanned for misconfigurations (Checkov, Trivy config, tfsec, hadolint, ...) on every change.\n\n**Risk:** Misconfigurations such as public buckets or open ports are the leading cause of cloud breaches, and are cheap to catch before apply.\n\n**Fix:** Run Checkov or Trivy in config mode on pull requests that touch infrastructure.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- run: trivy config --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL .\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC4.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.1/sast",
              "name": "Sast",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Static analysis (SAST) runs in CI"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A SAST tool (CodeQL, Semgrep, Bandit, gosec, ...) runs on every pull request."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable CodeQL (GitHub default setup or a workflow) or run Semgrep on pull requests.\n\nRisk: Injection and other code-level flaws reach production without automated review.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.1**: A SAST tool (CodeQL, Semgrep, Bandit, gosec, ...) runs on every pull request.\n\n**Risk:** Injection and other code-level flaws reach production without automated review.\n\n**Fix:** Enable CodeQL (GitHub default setup or a workflow) or run Semgrep on pull requests.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- uses: github/codeql-action/init@<sha>  # pin to a commit SHA\n  with: { languages: javascript-typescript }\n- uses: github/codeql-action/analyze@<sha>\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC4.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.1/scheduled-scans",
              "name": "ScheduledScans",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security scans also run on a schedule"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Security scans run on a schedule against the default branch, not only on pull requests, so new vulnerabilities in unchanged code are found."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a schedule trigger to the workflow that runs your scanners.\n\nRisk: A repository nobody touches is never rescanned, while new CVEs land against its dependencies every week.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.1**: Security scans run on a schedule against the default branch, not only on pull requests, so new vulnerabilities in unchanged code are found.\n\n**Risk:** A repository nobody touches is never rescanned, while new CVEs land against its dependencies every week.\n\n**Fix:** Add a schedule trigger to the workflow that runs your scanners.\n\nExample:\n\n```\non:\n  pull_request:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: '17 6 * * 1'  # weekly\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC4.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.2/blocking-scans",
              "name": "BlockingScans",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security scans block the pipeline on findings"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Security scan steps fail the pipeline on Critical/High findings instead of reporting and carrying on."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove continue-on-error / \"|| true\" from scan steps and set the scanner to fail on Critical and High.\n\nRisk: Non-blocking scanners produce reports nobody reads; known issues merge anyway.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.2**: Security scan steps fail the pipeline on Critical/High findings instead of reporting and carrying on.\n\n**Risk:** Non-blocking scanners produce reports nobody reads; known issues merge anyway.\n\n**Fix:** Remove continue-on-error / \"|| true\" from scan steps and set the scanner to fail on Critical and High.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- run: trivy fs --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL,HIGH .\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC4.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.2/remediation-sla",
              "name": "RemediationSla",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Remediation SLAs are defined by severity"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Remediation deadlines are defined per severity (e.g. Critical 7 days, High 30) and overdue findings are escalated."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Publish remediation SLAs per severity and an escalation path for overdue findings, and configure the same values in threesock.yaml.\n\nRisk: Without deadlines, known vulnerabilities age indefinitely, and auditors flag aged Critical/High findings as a control failure.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.2**: Remediation deadlines are defined per severity (e.g. Critical 7 days, High 30) and overdue findings are escalated.\n\n**Risk:** Without deadlines, known vulnerabilities age indefinitely, and auditors flag aged Critical/High findings as a control failure.\n\n**Fix:** Publish remediation SLAs per severity and an escalation path for overdue findings, and configure the same values in threesock.yaml.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Severity | Fix within |\n|---|---|\n| Critical | 7 days |\n| High | 30 days |\nOverdue findings are escalated to the engineering lead; exceptions need an approver and expiry.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC4.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc4.2/suppressions",
              "name": "Suppressions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner suppressions carry a justification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every suppression of a security finding says why (and ideally who approved it and until when)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add the rule id and a reason to each suppression, or move it into threesock.yaml exceptions with an approver and expiry.\n\nRisk: Unexplained suppressions hide real vulnerabilities and cannot be reviewed; auditors treat blanket suppressions as a red flag.",
                "markdown": "**CC4.2**: Every suppression of a security finding says why (and ideally who approved it and until when).\n\n**Risk:** Unexplained suppressions hide real vulnerabilities and cannot be reviewed; auditors treat blanket suppressions as a red flag.\n\n**Fix:** Add the rule id and a reason to each suppression, or move it into threesock.yaml exceptions with an approver and expiry.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nsubprocess.run(cmd, shell=True)  # nosec B602: cmd is a fixed literal, no user input (SEC-41)\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC4.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC4.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.1/control-matrix",
              "name": "ControlMatrix",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Controls are mapped to the risks they mitigate"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A control matrix (or a risk register with a controls column) maps each identified risk to the controls that address it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a controls column to the risk register, or keep a separate control matrix table.\n\nRisk: Controls that are not tied to risks drift into box-ticking, and uncovered risks go unnoticed.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.1**: A control matrix (or a risk register with a controls column) maps each identified risk to the controls that address it.\n\n**Risk:** Controls that are not tied to risks drift into box-ticking, and uncovered risks go unnoticed.\n\n**Fix:** Add a controls column to the risk register, or keep a separate control matrix table.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Risk | Controls |\n|---|---|\n| Malicious dependency | Lockfiles, minimumReleaseAge, dependency review |\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC5.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/actions-pinned",
              "name": "ActionsPinned",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GitHub Actions are pinned to commit SHAs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every third-party action is pinned to a full 40-character commit SHA (with the version in a comment), so a moved tag cannot change what runs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pin each action to the commit SHA of the release you reviewed, keep the version as a comment, and let Dependabot update the pins.\n\nRisk: Tags can be moved. A compromised action tag (as in the tj-actions/changed-files incident) runs attacker code with your workflow's secrets.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Every third-party action is pinned to a full 40-character commit SHA (with the version in a comment), so a moved tag cannot change what runs.\n\n**Risk:** Tags can be moved. A compromised action tag (as in the tj-actions/changed-files incident) runs attacker code with your workflow's secrets.\n\n**Fix:** Pin each action to the commit SHA of the release you reviewed, keep the version as a comment, and let Dependabot update the pins.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- uses: actions/checkout@11bd71901bbe5b1630ceea73d27597364c9af683 # v4.2.2\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/cloud-credentials",
              "name": "CloudCredentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CI authenticates to clouds with OIDC, not long-lived keys"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Workflows assume cloud roles through OIDC federation (id-token: write), so no long-lived cloud keys are stored as CI secrets."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Configure an OIDC trust between GitHub and your cloud, assume a scoped role in the workflow, and delete the stored keys.\n\nRisk: Long-lived cloud keys in CI leak through logs, forks and compromised actions, and stay valid until someone remembers to rotate them.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Workflows assume cloud roles through OIDC federation (id-token: write), so no long-lived cloud keys are stored as CI secrets.\n\n**Risk:** Long-lived cloud keys in CI leak through logs, forks and compromised actions, and stay valid until someone remembers to rotate them.\n\n**Fix:** Configure an OIDC trust between GitHub and your cloud, assume a scoped role in the workflow, and delete the stored keys.\n\nExample:\n\n```\npermissions:\n  id-token: write\n  contents: read\nsteps:\n  - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@<sha>  # pin to a commit SHA\n    with:\n      role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/deploy\n      aws-region: us-east-1\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/deploy-environments",
              "name": "DeployEnvironments",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Deploy jobs run in protected environments"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Jobs that deploy or publish run in a GitHub environment, so required reviewers, branch restrictions and environment-scoped secrets apply."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add `environment: production` to deploy jobs and configure required reviewers and deployment branch rules on that environment.\n\nRisk: Without an environment gate, anyone who can push to a triggering branch can deploy to production.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Jobs that deploy or publish run in a GitHub environment, so required reviewers, branch restrictions and environment-scoped secrets apply.\n\n**Risk:** Without an environment gate, anyone who can push to a triggering branch can deploy to production.\n\n**Fix:** Add `environment: production` to deploy jobs and configure required reviewers and deployment branch rules on that environment.\n\nExample:\n\n```\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    environment: production\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/pull-request-target",
              "name": "PullRequestTarget",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Privileged workflows never check out untrusted pull request code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Workflows triggered by pull_request_target or workflow_run do not check out or run code from the pull request."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use the pull_request trigger for anything that builds or tests PR code. Keep pull_request_target for labelling or commenting, without checking out the head.\n\nRisk: These triggers run with secrets and a write token. Checking out the PR head lets anyone who opens a pull request run code with them (\"pwn request\").",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Workflows triggered by pull_request_target or workflow_run do not check out or run code from the pull request.\n\n**Risk:** These triggers run with secrets and a write token. Checking out the PR head lets anyone who opens a pull request run code with them (\"pwn request\").\n\n**Fix:** Use the pull_request trigger for anything that builds or tests PR code. Keep pull_request_target for labelling or commenting, without checking out the head.\n\nExample:\n\n```\non: pull_request          # untrusted code, no secrets, read-only token\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/script-injection",
              "name": "ScriptInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Untrusted event data is not interpolated into shell scripts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "run: steps pass attacker-controllable values (issue titles, PR bodies, branch names) through environment variables, never `${{ }}` interpolation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Move the value into an environment variable and reference it as a quoted shell variable.\n\nRisk: A crafted issue title or branch name becomes shell code running in your workflow, with its token and secrets.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: run: steps pass attacker-controllable values (issue titles, PR bodies, branch names) through environment variables, never `${{ }}` interpolation.\n\n**Risk:** A crafted issue title or branch name becomes shell code running in your workflow, with its token and secrets.\n\n**Fix:** Move the value into an environment variable and reference it as a quoted shell variable.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- run: echo \"Title: $TITLE\"\n  env:\n    TITLE: ${{ github.event.issue.title }}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/secrets-in-logs",
              "name": "SecretsInLogs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets are never echoed in CI logs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Workflows never print secrets; they pass them to tools through environment variables or files."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the echo. Pass the secret through env and let the tool read it, or write it to a file with restricted permissions.\n\nRisk: Masking is best-effort: transformed or partial values slip through, and logs are readable by everyone with repository read access.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Workflows never print secrets; they pass them to tools through environment variables or files.\n\n**Risk:** Masking is best-effort: transformed or partial values slip through, and logs are readable by everyone with repository read access.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the echo. Pass the secret through env and let the tool read it, or write it to a file with restricted permissions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- run: ./deploy.sh\n  env:\n    DEPLOY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_TOKEN }}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.2/workflow-permissions",
              "name": "WorkflowPermissions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workflow tokens use least-privilege permissions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each workflow declares `permissions:` (read-only by default) and grants write scopes only to the jobs that need them. Nothing uses write-all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set a read-only default at the top of each workflow and grant specific write scopes per job.\n\nRisk: A broad GITHUB_TOKEN lets any compromised step push code, create releases or change repository settings.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.2**: Each workflow declares `permissions:` (read-only by default) and grants write scopes only to the jobs that need them. Nothing uses write-all.\n\n**Risk:** A broad GITHUB_TOKEN lets any compromised step push code, create releases or change repository settings.\n\n**Fix:** Set a read-only default at the top of each workflow and grant specific write scopes per job.\n\nExample:\n\n```\npermissions:\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  release:\n    permissions:\n      contents: write\n      id-token: write\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC5.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.3/policy-review",
              "name": "PolicyReview",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Policies record an owner and a review within the last year"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each policy states its owner and the date it was last reviewed, and is reviewed at least annually."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add an owner and a \"Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD\" line to each policy, and review them yearly.\n\nRisk: Stale or ownerless policies describe controls that no longer operate, which auditors test against.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.3**: Each policy states its owner and the date it was last reviewed, and is reviewed at least annually.\n\n**Risk:** Stale or ownerless policies describe controls that no longer operate, which auditors test against.\n\n**Fix:** Add an owner and a \"Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD\" line to each policy, and review them yearly.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nOwner: @security-lead\nLast reviewed: 2026-10-01\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC5.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc5.3/runbooks",
              "name": "Runbooks",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Procedures exist for the core operational processes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Runbooks cover onboarding, offboarding, deployment, rollback, incident response, key rotation and access reviews."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a section (heading) for each missing procedure to your runbooks.\n\nRisk: Undocumented procedures are performed inconsistently and cannot be shown to an auditor.",
                "markdown": "**CC5.3**: Runbooks cover onboarding, offboarding, deployment, rollback, incident response, key rotation and access reviews.\n\n**Risk:** Undocumented procedures are performed inconsistently and cannot be shown to an auditor.\n\n**Fix:** Add a section (heading) for each missing procedure to your runbooks.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndocs/runbooks/\n  access.md     # onboarding, offboarding, access reviews\n  release.md    # deployment and rollback\n  incidents.md  # incident response\n  secrets.md    # key rotation\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC5.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC5.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/cookie-flags",
              "name": "CookieFlags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session cookies are HttpOnly and Secure"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Session and auth cookies set HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set httpOnly, secure and sameSite on every session or auth cookie. Use an environment check only to relax Secure for local HTTP development.\n\nRisk: Without HttpOnly an XSS bug can steal sessions; without Secure the cookie can leak over plain HTTP.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: Session and auth cookies set HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite.\n\n**Risk:** Without HttpOnly an XSS bug can steal sessions; without Secure the cookie can leak over plain HTTP.\n\n**Fix:** Set httpOnly, secure and sameSite on every session or auth cookie. Use an environment check only to relax Secure for local HTTP development.\n\nExample:\n\n```\napp.use(session({\n  cookie: { httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'lax', maxAge: 8 * 60 * 60 * 1000 },\n}));\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/env-files",
              "name": "EnvFiles",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": ".env files are not committed and are gitignored"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Environment files with real values stay out of version control. Only templates such as .env.example are committed, and .gitignore excludes .env files."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Rotate any credentials the file contains, remove it from git with `git rm --cached`, add `.env*` to .gitignore, and commit a .env.example with empty values instead.\n\nRisk: A committed .env file usually holds live credentials, which are then exposed to everyone with repository access and remain in git history.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: Environment files with real values stay out of version control. Only templates such as .env.example are committed, and .gitignore excludes .env files.\n\n**Risk:** A committed .env file usually holds live credentials, which are then exposed to everyone with repository access and remain in git history.\n\n**Fix:** Rotate any credentials the file contains, remove it from git with `git rm --cached`, add `.env*` to .gitignore, and commit a .env.example with empty values instead.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# .gitignore\n.env\n.env.*\n!.env.example\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/generic-password",
              "name": "GenericPassword",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No literal passwords or keys assigned in code or config"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Credential-like settings read their values from the environment or a secrets manager, never from string literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace the literal with a value loaded at runtime. If it is not a credential, add an exception with a justification.\n\nRisk: A literal credential in code is shared with everyone who can read the repository and is hard to rotate. This check is heuristic, so confirm each match.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: Credential-like settings read their values from the environment or a secrets manager, never from string literals.\n\n**Risk:** A literal credential in code is shared with everyone who can read the repository and is hard to rotate. This check is heuristic, so confirm each match.\n\n**Fix:** Replace the literal with a value loaded at runtime. If it is not a credential, add an exception with a justification.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n// After\nconst client = new Client({ password: process.env.SMTP_PASSWORD });\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/hardcoded-secrets",
              "name": "HardcodedSecrets",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No secrets committed in source or config"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "No secrets in source, config files, images or history. Secrets are loaded at runtime from a secrets manager or environment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Revoke and rotate the credential first, then remove it from the code and load it from a secrets manager or environment variable. Rewriting git history does not un-leak a pushed secret.\n\nRisk: Anyone with read access to the repository, its forks or its history can use the credential. Leaked keys are routinely harvested within minutes of a public push.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: No secrets in source, config files, images or history. Secrets are loaded at runtime from a secrets manager or environment.\n\n**Risk:** Anyone with read access to the repository, its forks or its history can use the credential. Leaked keys are routinely harvested within minutes of a public push.\n\n**Fix:** Revoke and rotate the credential first, then remove it from the code and load it from a secrets manager or environment variable. Rewriting git history does not un-leak a pushed secret.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n// Before\nconst stripe = new Stripe('sk_live_...');\n\n// After\nconst stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY!);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/iam-wildcard",
              "name": "IamWildcard",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policies avoid wildcard actions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "IAM roles and policies grant specific actions on specific resources; no \"*\" actions and no primitive owner/editor roles."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace wildcards with the specific actions the workload needs. IAM Access Analyzer or CloudTrail-based policy generation can produce a least-privilege policy from real usage.\n\nRisk: A wildcard policy turns any compromise of that identity into full control of the account.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: IAM roles and policies grant specific actions on specific resources; no \"*\" actions and no primitive owner/editor roles.\n\n**Risk:** A wildcard policy turns any compromise of that identity into full control of the account.\n\n**Fix:** Replace wildcards with the specific actions the workload needs. IAM Access Analyzer or CloudTrail-based policy generation can produce a least-privilege policy from real usage.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndata \"aws_iam_policy_document\" \"app\" {\n  statement {\n    actions   = [\"s3:GetObject\", \"s3:PutObject\"]\n    resources = [\"${aws_s3_bucket.uploads.arn}/*\"]\n  }\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/jwt-verification",
              "name": "JwtVerification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWTs are verified with a strong algorithm and expiry"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "JWTs are signed with strong algorithms and validated (alg, exp, aud, iss) on every request."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Always verify tokens with an explicit algorithm allow-list, and check exp, aud and iss. Use decode only for logging-free inspection of already-verified tokens.\n\nRisk: An unverified or \"none\"-algorithm token lets anyone forge a session for any user.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: JWTs are signed with strong algorithms and validated (alg, exp, aud, iss) on every request.\n\n**Risk:** An unverified or \"none\"-algorithm token lets anyone forge a session for any user.\n\n**Fix:** Always verify tokens with an explicit algorithm allow-list, and check exp, aud and iss. Use decode only for logging-free inspection of already-verified tokens.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nconst claims = jwt.verify(token, publicKey, {\n  algorithms: ['RS256'], audience: 'api.example.com', issuer: 'https://auth.example.com/',\n});\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/password-hashing",
              "name": "PasswordHashing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Passwords hashed with Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Passwords are hashed with Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt (rubric 1.4). Fast hashes such as MD5, SHA-1 or SHA-256 are never used for passwords."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hash passwords with Argon2id (preferred), bcrypt or scrypt through a maintained library, and rehash on next login when migrating.\n\nRisk: Fast hashes can be brute-forced at billions of guesses per second, so a database leak becomes a credential leak.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: Passwords are hashed with Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt (rubric 1.4). Fast hashes such as MD5, SHA-1 or SHA-256 are never used for passwords.\n\n**Risk:** Fast hashes can be brute-forced at billions of guesses per second, so a database leak becomes a credential leak.\n\n**Fix:** Hash passwords with Argon2id (preferred), bcrypt or scrypt through a maintained library, and rehash on next login when migrating.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport argon2 from 'argon2';\nconst hash = await argon2.hash(password, { type: argon2.argon2id });\nconst ok = await argon2.verify(hash, candidate);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/public-datastore",
              "name": "PublicDatastore",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Databases are not publicly accessible"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Data stores sit in private networks, are not publicly reachable, and require authentication."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Turn off public accessibility, place the database in private subnets, and reach it through the application, a bastion or a private endpoint.\n\nRisk: A publicly reachable database is one leaked or weak password away from a full data breach, and is scanned for continuously.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: Data stores sit in private networks, are not publicly reachable, and require authentication.\n\n**Risk:** A publicly reachable database is one leaked or weak password away from a full data breach, and is scanned for continuously.\n\n**Fix:** Turn off public accessibility, place the database in private subnets, and reach it through the application, a bastion or a private endpoint.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  publicly_accessible    = false\n  db_subnet_group_name   = aws_db_subnet_group.private.name\n  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.db.id]\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.1/secret-scanning",
              "name": "SecretScanning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret scanning runs automatically"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A secret scanner runs on every push or pull request (and ideally as a pre-commit hook), with push protection enabled on the hosting platform."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add Gitleaks (or TruffleHog) to CI and to pre-commit, and enable GitHub secret scanning with push protection.\n\nRisk: Without automated scanning, a committed credential is found by an attacker before it is found by you.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.1**: A secret scanner runs on every push or pull request (and ideally as a pre-commit hook), with push protection enabled on the hosting platform.\n\n**Risk:** Without automated scanning, a committed credential is found by an attacker before it is found by you.\n\n**Fix:** Add Gitleaks (or TruffleHog) to CI and to pre-commit, and enable GitHub secret scanning with push protection.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# .github/workflows/secrets.yml\non: [push, pull_request]\npermissions: { contents: read }\njobs:\n  gitleaks:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha>  # pin to a commit SHA\n        with: { fetch-depth: 0 }\n      - uses: gitleaks/gitleaks-action@<sha>\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.2/mass-assignment",
              "name": "MassAssignment",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Users cannot assign their own roles through request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Create and update handlers copy only allow-listed fields from requests; roles and permissions are set by privileged code paths only."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Validate request bodies against a schema of allowed fields and set roles explicitly on the server.\n\nRisk: Mass assignment lets a user send `\"role\": \"admin\"` in a signup or profile update and become an administrator.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.2**: Create and update handlers copy only allow-listed fields from requests; roles and permissions are set by privileged code paths only.\n\n**Risk:** Mass assignment lets a user send `\"role\": \"admin\"` in a signup or profile update and become an administrator.\n\n**Fix:** Validate request bodies against a schema of allowed fields and set roles explicitly on the server.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nconst input = SignupSchema.parse(req.body); // { email, password, name } only\nawait User.create({ ...input, role: 'member' });\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.3/offboarding",
              "name": "Offboarding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An offboarding procedure revokes access within a deadline"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Offboarding revokes SSO, source control, cloud, CI/CD and vendor access within a set deadline (≤ 24h)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Document an offboarding checklist covering every system with access, and the deadline for completing it.\n\nRisk: Departed staff and contractors keep working credentials, a common source of incidents and an audit finding.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.3**: Offboarding revokes SSO, source control, cloud, CI/CD and vendor access within a set deadline (≤ 24h).\n\n**Risk:** Departed staff and contractors keep working credentials, a common source of incidents and an audit finding.\n\n**Fix:** Document an offboarding checklist covering every system with access, and the deadline for completing it.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Offboarding (within 24 hours)\n- [ ] Disable SSO account (cascades to apps via SCIM)\n- [ ] Remove from GitHub org and npm org\n- [ ] Revoke personal cloud credentials and tokens\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.3/token-expiry",
              "name": "TokenExpiry",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Issued tokens expire"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every token the application issues has an expiry, so access ends even if revocation fails."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set a short expiry on every issued token and use refresh tokens that can be revoked.\n\nRisk: A token without expiry stays valid forever; one leak is permanent access.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.3**: Every token the application issues has an expiry, so access ends even if revocation fails.\n\n**Risk:** A token without expiry stays valid forever; one leak is permanent access.\n\n**Fix:** Set a short expiry on every issued token and use refresh tokens that can be revoked.\n\nExample:\n\n```\njwt.sign(claims, key, { algorithm: 'RS256', expiresIn: '15m' });\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.5/managed-keys",
              "name": "ManagedKeys",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data stores use customer-managed keys that can be destroyed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Data stores are encrypted with customer-managed KMS keys (with a deletion window), so destroying the key renders all copies, including snapshots, unrecoverable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Encrypt data stores with a dedicated customer-managed KMS key with rotation enabled.\n\nRisk: With provider-owned keys, decommissioned data and stray snapshots stay readable; disposal relies on finding every copy.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.5**: Data stores are encrypted with customer-managed KMS keys (with a deletion window), so destroying the key renders all copies, including snapshots, unrecoverable.\n\n**Risk:** With provider-owned keys, decommissioned data and stray snapshots stay readable; disposal relies on finding every copy.\n\n**Fix:** Encrypt data stores with a dedicated customer-managed KMS key with rotation enabled.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_kms_key\" \"data\" {\n  enable_key_rotation     = true\n  deletion_window_in_days = 30\n}\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" {\n  kms_key_id = aws_kms_key.data.arn\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.5"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/cors-credentials",
              "name": "CorsCredentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CORS does not allow any origin with credentials"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Credentialed CORS is limited to an explicit allow-list of origins; wildcard or reflected origins are only used for public, credential-less APIs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace the wildcard or reflected origin with an explicit list of trusted origins, or drop credentials for truly public endpoints.\n\nRisk: Any website a user visits can make authenticated requests to the API and read the responses.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Credentialed CORS is limited to an explicit allow-list of origins; wildcard or reflected origins are only used for public, credential-less APIs.\n\n**Risk:** Any website a user visits can make authenticated requests to the API and read the responses.\n\n**Fix:** Replace the wildcard or reflected origin with an explicit list of trusted origins, or drop credentials for truly public endpoints.\n\nExample:\n\n```\napp.use(cors({ origin: ['https://app.example.com'], credentials: true }));\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/dangerous-execution",
              "name": "DangerousExecution",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No dynamic code execution or shell injection sinks"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Code never evaluates strings as code, never builds shell commands from strings, and never deserializes untrusted data with unsafe loaders."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Replace dynamic evaluation with explicit logic, pass command arguments as an array without a shell, and use safe loaders (json, yaml.safe_load).\n\nRisk: If any part of the evaluated string or command can be influenced by a user, the result is remote code execution.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Code never evaluates strings as code, never builds shell commands from strings, and never deserializes untrusted data with unsafe loaders.\n\n**Risk:** If any part of the evaluated string or command can be influenced by a user, the result is remote code execution.\n\n**Fix:** Replace dynamic evaluation with explicit logic, pass command arguments as an array without a shell, and use safe loaders (json, yaml.safe_load).\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# Python: argument list, no shell\nsubprocess.run([\"convert\", src, dst], check=True)\n\n// Node: execFile with arguments, no shell\nexecFile('convert', [src, dst]);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/open-admin-ports",
              "name": "OpenAdminPorts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Admin and database ports are not open to the internet"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Firewall rules never allow 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0 to admin ports (SSH, RDP) or data ports (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis, MongoDB, ...)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Restrict ingress to known CIDRs or security groups, and reach admin interfaces through a VPN, bastion, SSM Session Manager or an identity-aware proxy.\n\nRisk: Internet-exposed admin and database ports are scanned constantly and are a leading entry point for breaches and ransomware.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Firewall rules never allow 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0 to admin ports (SSH, RDP) or data ports (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis, MongoDB, ...).\n\n**Risk:** Internet-exposed admin and database ports are scanned constantly and are a leading entry point for breaches and ransomware.\n\n**Fix:** Restrict ingress to known CIDRs or security groups, and reach admin interfaces through a VPN, bastion, SSM Session Manager or an identity-aware proxy.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ningress {\n  from_port       = 5432\n  to_port         = 5432\n  protocol        = \"tcp\"\n  security_groups = [aws_security_group.app.id]  # not 0.0.0.0/0\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/public-buckets",
              "name": "PublicBuckets",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage buckets are not public"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Object storage is private by default, with public access blocks enabled; public content is served through a CDN with origin access control."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove public ACLs and allUsers bindings, enable the public access block, and serve intentionally public assets through a CDN with origin access control.\n\nRisk: Public buckets are one of the most common causes of large data exposures and are discovered by automated scanners.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Object storage is private by default, with public access blocks enabled; public content is served through a CDN with origin access control.\n\n**Risk:** Public buckets are one of the most common causes of large data exposures and are discovered by automated scanners.\n\n**Fix:** Remove public ACLs and allUsers bindings, enable the public access block, and serve intentionally public assets through a CDN with origin access control.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block\" \"uploads\" {\n  bucket                  = aws_s3_bucket.uploads.id\n  block_public_acls       = true\n  block_public_policy     = true\n  ignore_public_acls      = true\n  restrict_public_buckets = true\n}\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/security-headers",
              "name": "SecurityHeaders",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP security headers are set"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Responses set CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options and frame protections, in code or at the edge (CDN, proxy, platform config)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a security-headers middleware (helmet, Talisman, SecurityMiddleware) or set the headers in your proxy or platform configuration.\n\nRisk: Missing headers remove browser-side defences against XSS, clickjacking and protocol downgrade.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Responses set CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options and frame protections, in code or at the edge (CDN, proxy, platform config).\n\n**Risk:** Missing headers remove browser-side defences against XSS, clickjacking and protocol downgrade.\n\n**Fix:** Add a security-headers middleware (helmet, Talisman, SecurityMiddleware) or set the headers in your proxy or platform configuration.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport helmet from 'helmet';\napp.use(helmet({ hsts: { maxAge: 31536000, includeSubDomains: true } }));\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/sql-string-building",
              "name": "SqlStringBuilding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL queries are parameterized, not built from strings"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Queries use bound parameters or a query builder; user input is never concatenated or interpolated into SQL."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass values as bound parameters (placeholders) or use a tagged template or query builder that parameterizes for you.\n\nRisk: String-built SQL is the classic injection flaw: one unescaped value can read, change or delete the whole database.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: Queries use bound parameters or a query builder; user input is never concatenated or interpolated into SQL.\n\n**Risk:** String-built SQL is the classic injection flaw: one unescaped value can read, change or delete the whole database.\n\n**Fix:** Pass values as bound parameters (placeholders) or use a tagged template or query builder that parameterizes for you.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n// Node (pg)\nawait db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);\n\n# Python (psycopg)\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s\", (user_id,))\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.6/xss-sinks",
              "name": "XssSinks",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No unescaped HTML rendering sinks"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTML output is escaped by the framework; raw-HTML sinks are avoided or fed only sanitized content."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Render text through the framework's escaping. Where raw HTML is unavoidable, sanitize it with DOMPurify (or bleach) on the same line so the intent is visible.\n\nRisk: Rendering raw HTML that contains user input enables cross-site scripting: session theft and actions on the victim's behalf.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.6**: HTML output is escaped by the framework; raw-HTML sinks are avoided or fed only sanitized content.\n\n**Risk:** Rendering raw HTML that contains user input enables cross-site scripting: session theft and actions on the victim's behalf.\n\n**Fix:** Render text through the framework's escaping. Where raw HTML is unavoidable, sanitize it with DOMPurify (or bleach) on the same line so the intent is visible.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: DOMPurify.sanitize(html) }} />\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.6"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.6"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.7/plain-http-urls",
              "name": "PlainHttpUrls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Service URLs use HTTPS, not plain HTTP"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every external and internal service URL uses https://. Plain HTTP is limited to localhost and documentation examples."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Switch the URL to https:// and make sure the target service redirects HTTP to HTTPS with HSTS.\n\nRisk: Requests over plain HTTP can be read and modified in transit, including tokens and personal data.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.7**: Every external and internal service URL uses https://. Plain HTTP is limited to localhost and documentation examples.\n\n**Risk:** Requests over plain HTTP can be read and modified in transit, including tokens and personal data.\n\n**Fix:** Switch the URL to https:// and make sure the target service redirects HTTP to HTTPS with HSTS.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nconst API_URL = process.env.API_URL ?? 'https://api.example.com';\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.7"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.7"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.7/tls-verification",
              "name": "TlsVerification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TLS certificate verification is never disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every TLS client validates certificates and hostnames. Private CAs are trusted explicitly instead of turning validation off."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the override. For internal or self-signed services, trust the specific CA bundle instead.\n\nRisk: With validation off, anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or alter traffic, including credentials.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.7**: Every TLS client validates certificates and hostnames. Private CAs are trusted explicitly instead of turning validation off.\n\n**Risk:** With validation off, anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or alter traffic, including credentials.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the override. For internal or self-signed services, trust the specific CA bundle instead.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n// Node: trust a private CA instead of disabling checks\nhttps.request(url, { ca: fs.readFileSync('certs/internal-ca.pem') });\n\n# Python\nrequests.get(url, verify=\"certs/internal-ca.pem\")\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.7"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.7"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.7/weak-tls-crypto",
              "name": "WeakTlsCrypto",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Weak TLS versions and ciphers are disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "TLS endpoints and clients require at least the configured minimum version (default 1.2), and code uses modern ciphers (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Raise the minimum TLS version to 1.2 or 1.3 and replace legacy ciphers with an authenticated cipher such as AES-256-GCM.\n\nRisk: Legacy protocols and ciphers have practical attacks that expose traffic or encrypted data.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.7**: TLS endpoints and clients require at least the configured minimum version (default 1.2), and code uses modern ciphers (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305).\n\n**Risk:** Legacy protocols and ciphers have practical attacks that expose traffic or encrypted data.\n\n**Fix:** Raise the minimum TLS version to 1.2 or 1.3 and replace legacy ciphers with an authenticated cipher such as AES-256-GCM.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ntls.createServer({ minVersion: 'TLSv1.3', key, cert });\nconst cipher = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.7"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.7"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.7/webhook-signatures",
              "name": "WebhookSignatures",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Incoming webhooks verify their signatures"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every webhook handler verifies the sender signature over the raw body, with a constant-time comparison, before acting."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Verify the provider signature with its SDK (or an HMAC over the raw body with timingSafeEqual) before processing.\n\nRisk: Unsigned webhooks let anyone forge events such as \"payment succeeded\" or \"user verified\".",
                "markdown": "**CC6.7**: Every webhook handler verifies the sender signature over the raw body, with a constant-time comparison, before acting.\n\n**Risk:** Unsigned webhooks let anyone forge events such as \"payment succeeded\" or \"user verified\".\n\n**Fix:** Verify the provider signature with its SDK (or an HMAC over the raw body with timingSafeEqual) before processing.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nconst event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(rawBody, req.headers['stripe-signature'], process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET);\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.7"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.7"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/base-images",
              "name": "BaseImages",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container base images are pinned"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Base images use an explicit version tag, ideally pinned by digest, from a trusted registry."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pin base images to a version and digest, and let Dependabot or Renovate propose updates.\n\nRisk: An untagged or :latest base image changes underneath you, so builds are not reproducible and a compromised upstream image flows straight in.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Base images use an explicit version tag, ideally pinned by digest, from a trusted registry.\n\n**Risk:** An untagged or :latest base image changes underneath you, so builds are not reproducible and a compromised upstream image flows straight in.\n\n**Fix:** Pin base images to a version and digest, and let Dependabot or Renovate propose updates.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nFROM node:22.11.0-bookworm-slim@sha256:<digest>\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/container-scanning",
              "name": "ContainerScanning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container images are scanned in CI"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Images built from this repository are scanned (Trivy, Grype, Snyk, Docker Scout) before they are pushed or deployed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Scan each built image in CI and fail on Critical/High vulnerabilities with a fix available.\n\nRisk: Base images and OS packages carry their own vulnerabilities that dependency scanning of your code does not see.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Images built from this repository are scanned (Trivy, Grype, Snyk, Docker Scout) before they are pushed or deployed.\n\n**Risk:** Base images and OS packages carry their own vulnerabilities that dependency scanning of your code does not see.\n\n**Fix:** Scan each built image in CI and fail on Critical/High vulnerabilities with a fix available.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@<sha>  # pin to a commit SHA\n  with: { image-ref: 'app:${{ github.sha }}', severity: 'CRITICAL,HIGH', exit-code: '1', ignore-unfixed: true }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/curl-pipe-shell",
              "name": "CurlPipeShell",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No unverified remote scripts piped to a shell"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Downloaded tools and scripts are verified with a checksum or signature before they run."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Download to a file, verify its checksum or signature, then run it, or install from a package manager.\n\nRisk: Piping a remote script to a shell runs whatever the server (or anyone who compromises it) sends, inside your build.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Downloaded tools and scripts are verified with a checksum or signature before they run.\n\n**Risk:** Piping a remote script to a shell runs whatever the server (or anyone who compromises it) sends, inside your build.\n\n**Fix:** Download to a file, verify its checksum or signature, then run it, or install from a package manager.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ncurl -fsSLo install.sh https://example.com/install.sh\necho \"<sha256>  install.sh\" | sha256sum -c -\nsh install.sh\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/dependency-scanning",
              "name": "DependencyScanning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dependencies are scanned for known vulnerabilities"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Dependabot or Renovate keeps dependencies current, and a vulnerability scan (dependency review, OSV-Scanner, npm audit, pip-audit, govulncheck) runs in CI."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable Dependabot (or Renovate) and add a dependency scan to pull-request CI.\n\nRisk: Known-vulnerable dependencies are the most common way attackers get in, and they are only fixed if someone notices.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Dependabot or Renovate keeps dependencies current, and a vulnerability scan (dependency review, OSV-Scanner, npm audit, pip-audit, govulncheck) runs in CI.\n\n**Risk:** Known-vulnerable dependencies are the most common way attackers get in, and they are only fixed if someone notices.\n\n**Fix:** Enable Dependabot (or Renovate) and add a dependency scan to pull-request CI.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# .github/dependabot.yml\nversion: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: npm\n    directory: /\n    schedule: { interval: weekly }\n  - package-ecosystem: github-actions\n    directory: /\n    schedule: { interval: weekly }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/lockfiles",
              "name": "Lockfiles",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dependency lockfiles are committed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every dependency manifest has a committed lockfile (or hash-pinned requirements) so installs are reproducible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Generate the lockfile with your package manager, commit it, and install with the frozen/ci mode in CI.\n\nRisk: Without a lockfile, each install can pull different, possibly compromised, versions, and a build cannot be reproduced.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Every dependency manifest has a committed lockfile (or hash-pinned requirements) so installs are reproducible.\n\n**Risk:** Without a lockfile, each install can pull different, possibly compromised, versions, and a build cannot be reproduced.\n\n**Fix:** Generate the lockfile with your package manager, commit it, and install with the frozen/ci mode in CI.\n\nExample:\n\n```\npnpm install && git add pnpm-lock.yaml       # Node\nuv lock && git add uv.lock                    # Python\npip-compile --generate-hashes requirements.in # pip\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6.8/reproducible-installs",
              "name": "ReproducibleInstalls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CI and image builds install from the lockfile"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Builds install exactly what the lockfile says (npm ci, pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, pip --require-hashes)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use the lockfile-respecting install command in CI and Dockerfiles.\n\nRisk: `npm install` in CI can rewrite the lockfile and pull versions nobody reviewed.",
                "markdown": "**CC6.8**: Builds install exactly what the lockfile says (npm ci, pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, pip --require-hashes).\n\n**Risk:** `npm install` in CI can rewrite the lockfile and pull versions nobody reviewed.\n\n**Fix:** Use the lockfile-respecting install command in CI and Dockerfiles.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n# or\nRUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --prod\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC6.8"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.1/container-user",
              "name": "ContainerUser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Containers run as a non-root user"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The final stage of each Dockerfile switches to a non-root USER."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Create or use an unprivileged user and switch to it in the final stage.\n\nRisk: A process running as root turns any code execution bug into full control of the container and an easier escape to the host.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.1**: The final stage of each Dockerfile switches to a non-root USER.\n\n**Risk:** A process running as root turns any code execution bug into full control of the container and an easier escape to the host.\n\n**Fix:** Create or use an unprivileged user and switch to it in the final stage.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nRUN addgroup --system app && adduser --system --ingroup app app\nUSER app\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC7.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.1/drift-detection",
              "name": "DriftDetection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Infrastructure drift is detected on a schedule"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A scheduled `terraform plan` (or a drift detection service) reports changes made outside of code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Run `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` on a schedule and alert when it reports changes.\n\nRisk: Manual console changes silently reopen gaps that code review closed, and are invisible until something breaks.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.1**: A scheduled `terraform plan` (or a drift detection service) reports changes made outside of code.\n\n**Risk:** Manual console changes silently reopen gaps that code review closed, and are invisible until something breaks.\n\n**Fix:** Run `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` on a schedule and alert when it reports changes.\n\nExample:\n\n```\non: { schedule: [{ cron: '0 6 * * *' }] }\n...\n      - run: terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -lock=false\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "criterion": "CC7.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.1/vulnerability-monitoring",
              "name": "VulnerabilityMonitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dependencies are monitored continuously for new vulnerabilities"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "New vulnerabilities in existing dependencies are detected continuously (Dependabot/Renovate alerts or a scheduled scan)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enable Dependabot (with security updates) or Renovate, or run a dependency scan on a weekly schedule.\n\nRisk: Code that does not change still becomes vulnerable as CVEs are published; without monitoring nobody finds out.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.1**: New vulnerabilities in existing dependencies are detected continuously (Dependabot/Renovate alerts or a scheduled scan).\n\n**Risk:** Code that does not change still becomes vulnerable as CVEs are published; without monitoring nobody finds out.\n\n**Fix:** Enable Dependabot (with security updates) or Renovate, or run a dependency scan on a weekly schedule.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n# .github/dependabot.yml\nversion: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: npm\n    directory: /\n    schedule: { interval: weekly }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.1"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC7.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.2/alert-rules",
              "name": "AlertRules",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Alert rules are defined and routed to an owner"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Alerts for auth-failure spikes, error rates and infrastructure anomalies are defined as code and routed to on-call."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Define alert rules in code (Prometheus, CloudWatch, Datadog, Grafana) with routing to an on-call channel.\n\nRisk: Attacks and outages that nobody is alerted to run for hours or days.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.2**: Alerts for auth-failure spikes, error rates and infrastructure anomalies are defined as code and routed to on-call.\n\n**Risk:** Attacks and outages that nobody is alerted to run for hours or days.\n\n**Fix:** Define alert rules in code (Prometheus, CloudWatch, Datadog, Grafana) with routing to an on-call channel.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n- alert: LoginFailureSpike\n  expr: sum(rate(auth_login_failures_total[5m])) > 20\n  labels: { severity: page }\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.2/sensitive-logging",
              "name": "SensitiveLogging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credentials and request bodies are not logged"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Logs never contain passwords, tokens, authorization headers, full request bodies or card numbers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Log identifiers and outcomes, not payloads. Use a logger with redaction paths for anything sensitive.\n\nRisk: Logs are copied widely (log platforms, support tools, laptops). Credentials in them become a breach with a long tail.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.2**: Logs never contain passwords, tokens, authorization headers, full request bodies or card numbers.\n\n**Risk:** Logs are copied widely (log platforms, support tools, laptops). Credentials in them become a breach with a long tail.\n\n**Fix:** Log identifiers and outcomes, not payloads. Use a logger with redaction paths for anything sensitive.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nconst log = pino({ redact: ['req.headers.authorization', 'req.body.password'] });\nlog.info({ userId, outcome: 'login_failed' });\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.2"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.3/triage-criteria",
              "name": "TriageCriteria",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security event severity and triage criteria are documented"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Documented criteria say how a security event is classified, at what severity it becomes an incident, and how the triage outcome is recorded."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Document severity levels with examples, the threshold for declaring an incident, and where triage outcomes (true/false positive) are recorded.\n\nRisk: Without shared criteria, the same alert is ignored by one person and escalated by another, and real incidents are missed.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.3**: Documented criteria say how a security event is classified, at what severity it becomes an incident, and how the triage outcome is recorded.\n\n**Risk:** Without shared criteria, the same alert is ignored by one person and escalated by another, and real incidents are missed.\n\n**Fix:** Document severity levels with examples, the threshold for declaring an incident, and where triage outcomes (true/false positive) are recorded.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n| Severity | Criteria | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Confirmed data exposure or active exploitation | Leaked production key used by a third party |\nTriage outcome (true/false positive, incident yes/no) is recorded on the ticket.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.3"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "criterion": "CC7.3"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.4/incident-response-plan",
              "name": "IncidentResponsePlan",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An incident response plan defines roles, severity, escalation and communication"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An incident response plan defines roles, severity levels, escalation paths and how internal and external communication happens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Write docs/security/incident-response.md covering who leads, how severity is set, when to escalate, and who tells customers and regulators.\n\nRisk: During an incident, an undocumented process means slow, inconsistent response and missed notification deadlines.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.4**: An incident response plan defines roles, severity levels, escalation paths and how internal and external communication happens.\n\n**Risk:** During an incident, an undocumented process means slow, inconsistent response and missed notification deadlines.\n\n**Fix:** Write docs/security/incident-response.md covering who leads, how severity is set, when to escalate, and who tells customers and regulators.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Roles\nIncident lead: on-call engineer. Comms: head of support.\n## Severity\nSEV1: data exposure or full outage. SEV2: partial outage. SEV3: minor.\n## Escalation\nSEV1 pages the CTO within 15 minutes.\n## Communication\nStatus page within 30 minutes; customers notified within 72 hours of a confirmed breach.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "soc2",
                  "CC7.4"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "criterion": "CC7.4"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc7.4/incident-runbooks",
              "name": "IncidentRunbooks",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Runbooks exist for common incident scenarios"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Runbooks cover credential leaks, data exposure, compromised dependencies and outages/DDoS."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add a short runbook per scenario: how to detect it, contain it, eradicate it and who to tell.\n\nRisk: Improvising the first steps of a credential leak or supply-chain incident costs the hours that matter most.",
                "markdown": "**CC7.4**: Runbooks cover credential leaks, data exposure, compromised dependencies and outages/DDoS.\n\n**Risk:** Improvising the first steps of a credential leak or supply-chain incident costs the hours that matter most.\n\n**Fix:** Add a short runbook per scenario: how to detect it, contain it, eradicate it and who to tell.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n## Credential leak\n1. Revoke the credential at the provider.\n2. Search logs for use since the leak.\n3. Rotate dependent secrets and record the incident.\n```"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
